# Abstract

**Efficient Alignment of Frozen Vision and Language Encoders Under Tight Adaptation and Latency Budgets**

Contrastive vision–language models acquire their capability from jointly
training two large towers on web-scale paired data, at compute and data costs
incompatible with many research and deployment settings. This dissertation asks
whether independently pretrained vision and language encoders can instead be
aligned while frozen, using only a small trainable module under a budget of
fewer than five million trainable inference parameters and a measured latency
constraint. MobileCLIP2-S0 is the compact jointly pretrained reference;
OpenCLIP ViT-B/32 is the field anchor and same-allocation latency control, both
reconstructed locally so that checkpoints, transforms, splits and hardware are
matched rather than imported.

Early results were unexpectedly weak, so the first contribution is diagnostic
rather than architectural. Decomposing the training recipe isolated the
cross-batch memory queue — included to supply additional negatives — as the
dominant cause: although the encoders were frozen, queued embeddings were
stored downstream of a rapidly changing trainable projector, so historical
entries fell out of correspondence with the current model. Removing the queue
recovered roughly 19 percentage points of retrieval R@1 and motivated a
preregistered factorial over queue age, size and modality (22 conditions, 66
runs, three seeds). Harm began after a single optimizer step, no tested
non-zero age was safe, and matched-age comparisons excluded entry count as the
dominant explanation. Decisively, conditions with near-identical measured
projector drift differed in damage by up to 4.74 percentage points, and the
text tower's degradation response was approximately 7.2 times steeper than the
image tower's over the shared drift range. Drift is associated with damage but
does not determine it. This is the dissertation's principal scientific finding:
a boundary condition on the slow-drift justification for memory queues in
frozen cross-modal alignment, reported without a claim to the complete causal
mechanism.

With the recipe repaired, the remaining work asked how much performance was
recoverable under the efficiency constraint. Encoder-pair screening selected the
frozen towers; data experiments showed that unique in-domain coverage helped but
that added web data was not automatically beneficial, the bounded CC3M arm
failing to converge within its compute ceiling. Distillation transferred
information from MobileCLIP2-S0 at training time only. Architecturally,
learned-query aggregation over frozen token sequences substantially outperformed
mean pooling, showing that access to patch tokens is insufficient without
learned selection, and yielded M_T1, the strongest strictly frozen model at
54.487% validation mean bidirectional R@1 with 2.896M trainable inference
parameters. Internal token merging reduced latency but cost 5.69–15.95 points
across all configurations and was rejected rather than promoted. Guarded
hard-negative mining was likewise terminated before training when its
preregistered feasibility rule declared the candidate set infeasible, rather
than weakening the criterion after seeing the result.

The residual gap indicated a limit in the alignment head's reach rather than its
capacity: fully fixed encoders cannot adapt their representations to one
another. FreezeShift, a preregistered bounded relaxation applying low-rank
adaptation to the final encoder blocks, tested this. Adapting both towers was
strongest, raising validation R@1 from 54.49% to 63.42% with 4.862M trainable
inference parameters, and a corrected same-allocation latency study — replacing
measurements that had mixed padding regimes and profiling sessions, and had
wrongly rejected an unchanged control — confirmed the result stayed inside the
latency budget.

On the sealed one-shot Flickr30k test, evaluated after the roster was frozen,
strictly frozen M_T1 reached 52.90 ± 0.87% and dual FreezeShift 62.20 ± 0.45%
mean bidirectional R@1 across three seeds, giving a measured 9.30-point cost of
strict freezing. FreezeShift retained approximately 91.2% of OpenCLIP
ViT-B/32's retrieval performance at 32.5% of its total parameters, but only
79.5% of MobileCLIP2-S0's at 65.7% of its size. The conclusion is
correspondingly bounded: this work does not beat CLIP or reach compact-reference
parity. It establishes how far efficient alignment of independently pretrained
encoders can be pushed, identifies strict freezing as a measurable and
quantified bottleneck, and surfaces a more general training failure — stale
negatives can become harmful immediately, and in modality-dependent ways that
embedding drift alone does not explain.
